\section{Use a Target Set to Build Guarded Direct Calls}

Knowing several possible callee labels need not force a choice between a single unconditional direct call and wholly generic dispatch. A compiler can use a small target set as a menu of candidates for guarded direct calls. Assume a strict, left-to-right unary application and procedures with immutable code identities and an environment accessible from the closure descriptor. The optimizer receives candidate entries with compatible arity and a size budget; it emits at most that many label tests. The postcondition is the same result, effects, or call error as for ordinary application, not that every execution necessarily takes a direct branch.

\begin{verbatim}
emit_apply(operator, operand, entries, budget):
  fun = evaluate(operator)
  arg = evaluate(operand)
  # Use the ordinary callable check at this point.
  if not unary_callable(fun): raise CallError
  for entry in choose(entries, budget):
    if code_identity(fun) == entry.identity:
      # The label does not determine the environment.
      return direct(entry, environment(fun), arg)
  return generic_apply(fun, arg)
\end{verbatim}

Let two closures with label \texttt{addCaptured} hold captures \(3\) and \(8\); a third closure, with label \texttt{double}, doubles its operand. With one test for \texttt{addCaptured}, applying these closures in turn to \(5\) produces \(8,13,10\) on paths direct, direct, generic. The repeated match checks that each direct invocation forwards its own environment; baking in the first observed capture would give \(8\) on the second invocation. The fallback also works if a newly admitted library supplies an unexpected but valid closure. In a compiler where generic application handles errors itself, the initial check can be replaced by a safe descriptor guard and the fallback can retain the original error path.

Each guarded arm must implement the generic entry's semantics for every matching descriptor, including tagged argument and result conventions. Since there is a fallback, omitting an uncommon or unknown target from this menu loses performance, not behavior. Actual worst-case dispatch still executes every emitted comparison before the fallback, so a size budget and execution profile can matter more than ordering entries by their appearance in the analysis. The profile selects which tests to emit; it never proves them true.
